p=A.cK(A.a0(A.a([l.aBp(a,"Build",i,l.boJ(a)),B.aoW,l.byp(a,"Engine",u.z),B.pJ,A.p("What's new",k,k,k,k,k,g.x,k,k,k),B.bp,A.p('\u2022 Korea travel access: Travel access now renders as a true heat map \u2014 the Reach Explorer\'s Heat toggle is a real continuously-blended gradient (no more grid-cell edges), maps now stay locked to north (no more accidental rotate/tilt), and panning the map no longer scrolls the page underneath it.\n\u2022 Dispatch (new): set project rules right in the app \u2014 a group row\'s own Rules panel now has an editable rulesActive/rulesVersion/rules record editor (was API-only), with Recompute rules right alongside to refresh inheritance after you save.\n\u2022 Korea travel access: the Reach Explorer is more compact on phone \u2014 the 8-hub picker is now a single row of tappable chips (swipe to switch hubs too), and the tier legend collapses under the map behind a "Reach tiers" tap-to-expand toggle, so the map itself gets much more of the screen.\n\u2022 Dispatch (new): a project\'s overview gained a Plan chip \u2014 goals, milestones and decisions for that project, grouped and scoped to just its own subtree (previously a separate whole-board Plan tab). Project files gained a "Generate spec" action, and How dispatch works moved to the app\'s \u22ee menu.\n\u2022 Korea travel access: the Reach Explorer\'s Buckets/Heat toggle \u2014 a continuous blue-to-gray access-time gradient, not just 8 flat tiers \u2014 now works on all 8 origin hubs (Seoul Station, City Hall, Gangnam, Wangsimni, Sadang, Jamsil, Konkuk, Hongik), previously only on Seoul Station.\n\u2022 Dispatch: redesigned the Add-filter menu \u2014 tap-to-select checklists for status/lane/tag columns, a simple "contains\u2026" field for text, plain range fields for numbers and dates, and a "Filters:" chip row with a \u2715 per chip and a Clear all. Save a combination as a preset and reopen it in one tap.\n\u2022 Dispatch: tap an empty area of a list to clear the current selection.\n\u2022 Dispatch: fixed choppy scrolling in an embedded Priorities view (like a project\'s detail pane) \u2014 it now scrolls smoothly with the rest of the page instead of fighting its own inner scrollbar.\n\u2022 Fixed a bug where "Dispatch (new)" could show a project as unshared \u2014 no members, invites rejected \u2014 even though the original Dispatch already had it shared, because the two looked up shares under different internal ids.\n\u2022 Fixed a bug where updating Dispatch could re-insert its old demo tasks and links into a real, already-in-use board. A one-time cleanup now removes any of those phantom demo items it finds, and updates can no longer refill a board that already has real content in it.\n\u2022 New: "Dispatch (new)" \u2014 a side-by-side comparison applet reading the same board data as Dispatch, so you can try the newer navigation (Projects/Inbox/Priorities/Tags) against the original tab layout before we retire one. Dispatch itself is unchanged.\n\u2022 Dispatch: Priorities can focus on a selected project \u2014 tap \u2715 to see everything.\n\u2022 Dispatch: cleaner navigation \u2014 project views live on each project. Removed the Map, Plan, Spec, People and How-to tabs from the bottom bar (Map duplicated the per-project Bublie view; the rest were undesigned long lists).\n\u2022 Dispatch: Priorities can scope to a selected project \u2014 pick a project in Projects and the Priorities tab narrows to just its tasks, with a clearable "Scoped to: <project> \u2715" chip.\n\u2022 Dispatch: sub-projects now inherit their parent project\'s rules \u2014 a small shield badge marks any task or project whose effective rules include something from above it; the Rules panel shows the combined text and a "Recompute rules" button refreshes it after an edit.\n\u2022 Projects: attach files, diagrams and docs at the project level.\n\u2022 Dispatch: open a project and switch its view \u2014 Priorities, Bublie, or List \u2014 right from the project detail pane, scoped to just that project\'s tasks and its nested sub-projects, including the Bublie map.\n\u2022 Dispatch: projects can now show a color stripe + emoji on their tasks, in both the tree and the Priorities view.\n\u2022 Priorities: new Immediate lane + numeric ranking.\n\u2022 Open standard draw.io files directly \u2014 compressed saves now import.\n\u2022 Diagram and Flow \u2014 new Export/Import buttons let you save a diagram or flow to a file and bring it back later. Importing a compressed .drawio save now shows a clear "unsupported" message instead of failing silently.\n\u2022 Notes \u2014 Obsidian-style wiki-links. Write [[Note Title]] in a note and it becomes a real link; a new backlinks panel on each note shows what links to it, and a graph screen maps every note and link at once.\n\u2022 bublee polish switched on \u2014 the Feel section (wave-strength/range sliders) in the style bar, six new chain colour themes, and folds you collapse now stay collapsed after you leave and come back.\n\u2022 Fixed a bug where an app screen\'s own status banner (like a low-storage warning) could get stuck queued behind the "Add to Home Screen" hint and never show, or get dismissed by the wrong screen closing.\n\u2022 Fixed a files-library import/export gap in Docs and Notes: pages/notes could lose their internal folder structure when exporting an applet to a file and re-importing it.\n\u2022 krtravel Reach \u2014 areas outside your time budget now show as neutral grey instead of disappearing when you narrow the slider, and the tab\'s caption is upfront about the 8 fixed origin stations it covers.\n\u2022 krtravel Reach \u2014 the time-band legend now wraps to two rows on a phone instead of clipping when all 8 bands are shown.\n\u2022 Trip directions. Set a destination and get the fastest transit itinerary, step by step \u2014 starting in Boston, Toronto, Chicago, Helsinki and New York City. Also: map pins stay readable when you zoom out (nearby stops now group into clusters instead of piling up), address search feels smoother, translations are complete for the newest labels across all 55 supported languages with a permanent check to keep it that way, messages are clearer when an area can\'t load or is too large, and the world map grows with Melbourne and Athens, both with full-length route lines.\n\u2022 Phone polish. With 4\u20135 tabs the bottom bar now shows just the current tab\'s name so the labels stop getting squeezed. And the live-transit filters stay visible on a desktop \u2014 they only tuck away on a phone or a narrow panel, where a recent change had wrongly hidden them.\n\u2022 Dispatch Map: tap a bubble and choose Collapse cascade to fold everything that follows it along the arrows \u2014 tap again to unfold.\n\u2022 Screen Composer \u2014 new suite tool: drop widgets onto phone/tablet/web screens and they auto-fill real layout slots; screens link into click-through flows; boards save as files.\n\u2022 Maker \u2014 two modes. The maker now opens as Design | Develop: Design holds Wireframe, Flow and your Brief; Develop holds Data, Bind, Logic, Code and Build \u2014 switch modes from the app\'s tab control.\n\u2022 Maker \u2014 a real schema editor. On Develop \u2192 Data, every field is edited right in the table \u2014 name, type, options, required, and which table it belongs to.\n\u2022 Maker \u2014 app code view. Develop \u2192 Code shows your whole app as editable JSON: Generate from design fills it, edit freely, and Build from this code rebuilds the app.\n\u2022 Maker \u2014 group your specifics. The Brief\'s specifics list can group by priority, kind or screen again (the Group by pills are back).\n\u2022 Boxes: fixed a bug where a newly-created box could show another box\'s items while that box appeared empty \u2014 items are safe, this was a display/linking fix.\n\u2022 Boxes: one clean header row \u2014 new-box, search, filter, and view together.\n\u2022 Pull down on the home screen to refresh your account\'s applets and settings \u2014 and the update-available dot now also marks "Download latest app" in the \u22ee menu, not just "View app version".\n\u2022 Priority your tasks: Now / Next / Queued / Later \u2014 assign from the \u22ee menu, filter to just Now+Next, and the Priorities tab lanes by it.\n\u2022 Sort any list by title, created, edited, or owner.\n\u2022 Your data is always available offline \u2014 downloading only ever happens on first sign-in.\n\u2022 Open details on any folder from its \u22ee menu.\n\u2022 Filter from the root Projects list.\n\u2022 Saved views \u2014 save filter combinations and reopen them in one tap (Views row).\n\u2022 See what\'s new before you update \u2014 a quiet dot now appears on the \u22ee menu (and next to "View app version") when a new build is ready, and the version screen shows exactly what changed and an Update now button, right there. No pop-ups.\n\u2022 New Priorities tab \u2014 see what\'s in motion now, what\'s pending, and what\'s future, by project or flat.\n\u2022 Back button now steps up one level at a time \u2014 and asks before leaving.\n\u2022 Editing is smoother \u2014 saves write only what changed.\n\u2022 Download the Android app from View app version \u2014 the \u22ee menu\'s About window now has a direct APK download button on every platform, web included, so a cached web tab can always jump straight to the native app instead.\n\u2022 Get the app \u2014 download links in View app version. Tap the small icon next to the build number for a popup listing macOS, iPhone, Android and Windows; Android\'s direct APK link works today (the rest are coming), reachable on every platform, web included, so a cached web tab can always jump straight to the native app.\n\u2022 Dispatch opens fast \u2014 only new changes download, not the whole project. Fixed two spots where a quick close right after a background sync tick could leave the "what\'s new since last time" bookmark unsaved, so the next open silently re-fetched everything again.\n\u2022 Filter your task list \u2014 date ranges, stacked filters, match all/any.\n\u2022 Pin projects and tasks to the top; pins persist per app.\n\u2022 Refresh a shared project directly from its row menu \u2014 open a shared project\'s Settings and tap "Refresh this project" to pull just that project\'s latest changes, without waiting for every other shared project to re-sync too.\n\u2022 Smoother window switching \u2014 background checks no longer run on every focus.\n\u2022 Shared-project enrollment now works in every applet, not just the Dispatch board \u2014 a fix that could leave a device never picking up an invitation to a shared project outside Dispatch.\n\u2022 Refresh now is honest about empty projects \u2014 if there\'s nothing shared yet it says "Not connected to any shared project" instead of claiming "Up to date" without checking anything.\n\u2022 Refresh now \u2014 force a full re-sync from the server (app-bar \u21bb and Sync settings).\n\u2022 Opening a project in card view now expands it in the directory tree.\n\u2022 Fixed a sync bug: shared-project changes from a collaborator \u2014 moving, archiving, or reorganizing items \u2014 now appear immediately instead of needing a restart to show up.\n\u2022 Convert a task into a project (or back) from the row menu \u2014 turn a task into a project when it grows sub-items, or collapse an empty project back into a plain task.\n\u2022 Boards open even faster now \u2014 returning to a project with a lot of notes/comments no longer decodes them until you actually open a thread; only the structure (tasks, groups, files) loads up front.\n\u2022 Mobile: sub-projects/tasks now open as a directory with tappable cards, and boards open faster via lazy log loading.\n\u2022 Systemwide back button on your phone \u2014 the hardware/gesture back button now correctly unwinds wherever you\'ve drilled in (Explorer folders, a Dispatch bublee zoom) one level at a time, instead of sometimes jumping straight out of the applet.\n\u2022 Faster editing \u2014 renaming, adding, and deleting items in the explorer no longer lags; the engine now only recomputes the file/folder tree when a change can actually reshape it, instead of on every keystroke.\n\u2022 Settings moved into the right-click (\u22ee) menu on a project, next to Sharing and Files \u2014 one place for both.\n\u2022 Shared-project updates now arrive instantly instead of waiting for the next sync tick \u2014 a collaborator\'s edit shows up live.\n\u2022 Shared projects now sync automatically once shared \u2014 no manual Project-sharing toggle needed. Before, even after a project was shared, you had to find and flip a separate Sync settings switch before edits would actually flow between owner and collaborator; that switch is now just an optional override, and sharing itself turns syncing on.\n\u2022 Project sharing \u2014 fixed a gap where a project\'s owner could end up never seeing their collaborator\'s edits (and their own edits never reaching the collaborator) if the one-time server step that enrolls the owner didn\'t run. Owners now sync their own shared projects straight from being the owner, with no dependency on that extra step having happened.\n\u2022 Project sharing \u2014 invite by username now actually works. Fixed a bug where inviting a real account (like the Claude support account) to a shared project failed with "no account found" even though the account existed \u2014 the lookup was silently blocked by a privacy rule instead of reporting the real answer. It now resolves correctly through a small, privacy-scoped server check (only confirms an exact username exists, nothing else about that account). Sharing itself is still opt-in and off by default while it finishes rolling out.\n\u2022 Project Settings \u2014 selecting a project now shows a settings gear next to it; Settings gathers Sharing (member list, invite, share-copy) and a new Files section (export project / import here / merge a shared file / view invitations) in one place, out of the project\'s \u22ee menu, which is shorter now. Sharing itself stays in a calm "coming soon" state until the server-side rollout finishes; performance work behind the scenes made bulk project shares much faster to set up.\n\u2022 Dispatch\'s Map tab is now bublie itself, opened on your Dispatch map file \u2014 fold branches, arrange freely, it\'s a real board.\n\u2022 Dispatch: Map in bublee \u2014 your projects and tasks as a real bublee board, with fold-away branches.\n\u2022 College guide & Events: tap a card\'s "Show on map" button to jump to the Map tab centered on that campus or event (pin highlighted). "Show all" zooms back out.\n\u2022 Wide tables read properly on a phone now. In Workboard and Atlas, the tables with lots of columns (tasks, runs, layers) switch to one tidy card per row on a phone \u2014 every field on its own line, tap a card to open the row \u2014 instead of making you scroll sideways. On a tablet or desktop they stay normal grids, unchanged.\n\u2022 Dispatch map: bublee-style hold menu \u2014 rename in place and zoom in on a bubble \u2014 plus status colours matching the task list (active/queued/done).\n\u2022 Tree views keep your scroll position when expanding or collapsing.\n\u2022 Task list: jump up to the parent project \u2014 the task list header now has an up-arrow (tooltip names the parent) plus a tappable breadcrumb trail, and the same tap-to-jump breadcrumb now works in the task detail view too. The task view can also be closed \u2014 a close (\xd7) button clears the selection and returns you to the task list.\n\u2022 Big boards feel light now. Two speed-ups for large applets like a full Dispatch: lists and project trees only draw the rows on your screen (hundreds of off-screen rows no longer render on every tap), and clicking around no longer re-saves the whole board when nothing changed \u2014 selecting, expanding, and navigating are smooth even at many hundreds of items.\n\u2022 Get the app \u2014 Settings now has direct Download for macOS / Android buttons under "Get the app", so you don\'t need to hunt for a link to install isan on another device.\n\u2022 Transit \u2014 distinct modes with their own toggles. Train, tram + streetcar, bus, and express bus now show/hide separately, so you can clear the map down to just rail, or just buses, in one tap. Type a route number into the search box to isolate just that route. The map itself is simpler too: a fixed 5-mile view centered on your pin \u2014 no more radius dial to fiddle with. Route lines are cleaner when you zoom in: duplicate/overlapping lines are removed, local buses fade so rail stays readable, and whichever route you have selected always stays bold. And you can now save a view (filters + location) with a name and jump straight back to it later.\n\u2022 Models \u2014 a new "Transit access (Korea, 45-min reach)" heatmap: ranks every South Korean subway station by how many OTHER stations it can reach within a 45-minute network trip, and Korea model maps are now colored by that reach band so the best- and worst-connected areas jump out at a glance.\n\u2022 Performance: Explorer list, tree, and map views are now O(1) instead of O(N\xb2) \u2014 large datasets (hundreds of rows) no longer cause lag when scrolling or switching views. Row lookups in the data store are also O(1) (indexed by id) so editing and filtering scale with data size.\n\u2022 Databoard polish: drag panels to reorder (new handle on each panel header); per-panel editor pencil (rename, switch view, template, size slider, order, show/hide) \u2014 edits persist and survive reload; panels always stay inside their frames on desktop, phone, and tabs (fixes the map painting over panels below); clear empty states with one-tap Clear filters when a filter or slice matches nothing; shared data controls (filter/group/sort/range/segment/search) persist across restarts. Geolabs + Datalab both carry the new panel tools.\n\u2022 Models \u2014 tap a model to open it (no more hold-down), and it opens ON its flow diagram: just that model\'s steps as a bubble-and-arrow map. Steps with inner detail wear a +N badge \u2014 tap one to zoom INSIDE it and see its own input \u2192 processing \u2192 output chain (the Korea models\' network-build steps drill for real now). Tap an arrow to see what flows along it, pinch to zoom, and use "View output on map" to jump straight to the model\'s Seoul heatmap/rankings. Summary, components, layers and data live in tabs beside the diagram. The separate Pipeline tab is gone \u2014 each model carries its own diagram; the global Inputs and Stacks lists moved into Registries.\n\u2022 Phone polish across the whole app frame. On a phone an applet\'s own buttons stay right where you expect them in the title bar; only the generic undo/redo (and Edit) slide into the \u22ee menu to make room, so nothing ever gets squeezed off. The floating tabs menu (for apps with lots of tabs) now scrolls instead of running off-screen, the long \u22ee menu groups its backup/reset actions apart, the Appearance theme switch fits on narrow screens, and the home menu opens as a tidy sheet. Docs and the live-transit filters also fold away neatly when their panel is narrow, not just on a phone.\n\u2022 Models \u2014 two new Korea models joined the registry: transit-network CENTRALITY (ranks every subway station and bus stop by degree / betweenness / closeness / PageRank on the stop-connection graph) and a travel-time HEATMAP from any origin across Korea (RAPTOR routing \u2192 isochrone bands). Both fully sourced (data.go.kr / Seoul Open Data / OSM) and honest about key-gated feeds. The data-foundation pipeline now shows its intermediate processing \u2014 geo averaging \u2192 geo smoothing \u2014 as an editable sub-chain, matching the drawn model.\n\u2022 Send any applet to your phone. Every applet\'s \u22ee menu now has "Send to phone\u2026" \u2014 a QR code (made right on your device, nothing is sent anywhere) plus the applet\'s link. Point the phone\'s camera at it and the applet opens in the browser, installing itself from the catalog if needed. A one-time hint then shows how to keep it on the home screen (iOS Safari: Share \u2192 Add to Home Screen \xb7 Android Chrome: \u22ee \u2192 Add to Home screen). Add &focus=1 to a link to open the applet filling the whole app, without the back-to-home arrow. And if the web app ever gets stuck on an old version, it now notices and offers a one-tap Reload.\n\u2022 Models \u2014 the flow diagram is now the map. On the Pipeline tab, tap any process bubble to open it (its math, inputs, outputs, swap options), tap an ARROW to see exactly what flows along it, pinch to zoom around the chain, and hold a bubble for the quick-action ring. Functions can be attached to any model as pre- or post-processors: each model\'s layers now read pre \u2192 core \u2192 post, and Catalog \u2192 Algorithm layers has an "Attach a function" button \u2014 pick the model, the method, the stage, done.\n\u2022 Models \u2014 every model now says where it runs (its server / provider) right on the model card, groundwork for choosing your own servers and AI models per applet later.\n\u2022 Wireframe on a phone \u2014 the whole canvas is yours now. The column of view buttons down the edge (zoom, pan, snap, labels, 3D, grid) tucks into one small dot, just like the tool palette already did \u2014 tap it when you need them. On tablet and desktop nothing changes.\n\u2022 Next service (transit) on a phone \u2014 the time slider and train/bus filters now fold behind one slim bar under the map, and the bar always shows what is active ("Live now \xb7 All"), so a filter you set is never hidden. Tap it to open the controls; the map and departures keep the screen.\n\u2022 Screenshot markup on a phone \u2014 same treatment: both tool rows tuck behind a "Tools" bar with undo and redo kept one tap away.\n\u2022 Under the hood, every tool now shares ONE way of doing this (and new tools get it automatically), so the phone behaviour stays consistent across Sketchpad, Notes, Docs, Sheets, Wireframe, transit and markup.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 exporting a project now takes your wireframes with it. Before, \u201cExport project\u201d saved the tasks and comments but left every wireframe, doc, sheet and sketch behind, so importing it elsewhere gave you empty ones. Their contents now travel inside the project file, and import puts them back.\n\u2022 Releases \u2014 see what each site is serving. A new read-only Releases applet (Developer) reads the live build stamp from the production and dev sites side by side, so "did my change land, and is dev ahead of production?" is answerable in the app instead of at a git prompt. It holds no credentials and cannot deploy anything.\n\u2022 Pop-up messages tidy themselves. Little notifications now always appear and then fade on their own \u2014 no more messages that stick around until you dismiss them. The timer bar only shows on ones with an Undo, so you can see how long you have.\n\u2022 Models \u2014 open a model, see its anatomy. Tap a model card and the drill-in now lists its algorithm layers (grouped active / selectable / planned, each tied to the Methods registry), what data flows in and out, and which model it builds on. Layers are rows \u2014 add, swap, or remove one without touching the rest, and the calibration solvers appear as the selectable teal-striped case.\n\u2022 Search works everywhere. The header search in Dispatch now filters the projects/tasks directory too (not just the log) \u2014 type to narrow the whole view at once.\n\u2022 Models \u2014 pick your calibration method. The calibration solver is now a swappable module: the Run setup card offers bisection (today\'s default), IPF (iterative proportional fitting / Furness) and Bayesian optimization, and whichever you pick is frozen onto each run\'s snapshot. Both new methods are documented in the Methods registry \u2014 what they do, when to use them, and what each must log to stay reproducible.\n\u2022 Open an applet directly by link: add ?applet=<id> to the web URL.\n\u2022 Search from the top. Dispatch now has a search in the app header (tap the magnifier) that filters the open task\u2019s log \u2014 one search box up top instead of a separate one inside the log. The header can hold custom buttons per app too, laying groundwork for search across every applet.\n\u2022 Select all with Cmd/Ctrl+A. Click in a project/task directory, then press Cmd+A (\u2318A) to select everything visible \u2014 quick for bulk moves or deletes.\n\u2022 Toasts now show a countdown. The little pop-up messages (like \u201cMoved to Trash\u201d) have a thin bar that runs down so you can see when they\u2019ll disappear \u2014 and they clear themselves.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 a tidier directory. Attachments (wireframes, files, sketches) no longer clutter the project/task tree on the left \u2014 they live with their task, in its log. The tree shows just projects and tasks.\n\u2022 Enter to confirm. When a confirm prompt appears (like \u201cDelete permanently?\u201d), pressing Enter now confirms it and Esc cancels \u2014 no reaching for the mouse. Works across the app.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 Tree or Cards, your choice. A switch at the top of the projects column flips it between the nested outline (Tree) and a card browser of the current level (Cards). In Cards, tapping a project opens its tasks in the column beside it, and a \u2304/\u2303 arrow on a card peeks its contents in place. Phones open in Cards by default.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 tap a project name to open it, tap again to close it (the name now toggles, not just opens).\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 bigger inline preview. A wireframe or sketch preview in the log is taller now \u2014 usable at a glance without going full-screen.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 fold anything, the same way. A task with sub-tasks and a comment with replies now both show the same little arrow a project folder has \u2014 tap it to collapse or expand. One consistent control across projects, tasks, and the log. (A Tree/Cards view switch is coming next.)\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 a new Spec tab. Tap a project and Dispatch writes a full spec of it \u2014 the whole task/sub-task outline, the materials attached to each, and the notes \u2014 as one document you can Copy or Download. It reads like a plan a coding session can pick up and carry out.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 tighter log, less scrolling. Who wrote a comment and when now sits on the SAME line as the comment (chat-style), and a comment\u2019s files and tools share one row instead of stacking \u2014 so more of the conversation fits on screen.\n\u2022 Datalens \u2014 one dashboard that reads data, compares it, and paints it on a map. Import a CSV (or edit the seeded rows), add type-checked filters, save slices, and contrast scenarios with %-change deltas \u2014 then open the Map and pick WHICH column the towns are shaded by with one tap; tapping a town filters every other view to it.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 moving a selection is a true drag now. As you drag, the selected area lifts and follows your finger (leaving a clean hole behind), so you can see exactly where it lands \u2014 no more guessing until you let go.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 scroll and zoom with the trackpad. Slide two fingers to move around the canvas in any direction, and pinch to zoom in and out.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 undo now catches moving things. Moving a selection, a fill, a gradient, clearing a layer, or placing a pasted image can all be undone and redone \u2014 one history covers everything.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 room to write. The comment box opens three lines tall and grows as you type, and editing now looks exactly like reading \u2014 same spot, same layout, nothing jumps.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 previews that show your work. A wireframe or sketch preview now frames the drawing itself (not an empty corner of the canvas), and every preview can fold to a small chip \u2014 one header button collapses or expands the whole log at once.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 comments are just comments. No title field when writing one, and tapping a comment puts the cursor right in the text, ready to type.\n\u2022 Dispatch on phone \u2014 predictable navigation. Opening the applet now lands on the Projects list; tapping a project shows its tasks, and only tapping a task opens its log. No more jumping straight into a task\'s entries. (Desktop keeps the three-panel view with the first task pre-selected.)\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 attachments live INSIDE the log now. A wireframe, doc, sheet, image or sketch added at the log level shows as a proper entry card \u2014 with who added it and when \u2014 in its place in the conversation, not as a loose tile at the bottom.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 SEARCH your comments. A search box sits above a task\u2019s log: type to filter the thread to entries whose comment or author matches, so you can find an important note fast. Clear it to see everything again.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 edit a wireframe or doc right in the comment. Attachments now open as a live, editable preview inside the entry \u2014 sketch or write without leaving the log \u2014 with an Expand button to go full-screen and shrink back.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 attachments show in the directory too. A task\u2019s wireframes, docs, sheets, images and sketches now appear under it in the left tree, so you can see and open them without opening the log.\n\u2022 Edit a note and start typing \u2014 opening a note or comment to edit now drops the cursor straight into the text (at the end), no extra tap. (Used by Dispatch logs and anywhere you edit a text field.)\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 every attachment keeps its OWN data. Each wireframe, sheet or doc you add is its own separate file, with a safety guard so two can never share data by accident.\n\u2022 Directory (Dispatch) \u2014 tidier moving, and folders that stay put. You can now pull any item out to the TOP level so it sits in no project at all \u2014 use its \u22ee menu \u2192 "Move to top level", or drag it onto the "Drag here for top level" strip at the foot of the list. While you drag, a LINE shows exactly where it will land: drop it above or below a row to place it there, or ONTO a folder to tuck it inside. And selecting a project by its name now just opens it and keeps it open \u2014 only the little arrow (chevron) closes a folder, so picking a project to work in can\u2019t accidentally collapse it.\n\u2022 Sketchpad, Notes & Docs on a phone \u2014 the tool bars now tuck away. On a narrow screen the drawing or formatting controls collapse behind a single "Tools" / "Format" bar so the canvas or page gets almost the whole screen; tap it to open them, tap again to hide. (In Sketchpad, undo, redo and Save stay pinned so the common actions never need a tap.) One shared pattern across the tools now \u2014 wireframe and the rest follow. On tablet and desktop nothing changes.\n\u2022 Dispatch directory \u2014 reorganise projects and tasks by hand. Drag an item and a line shows exactly where it will land (above, between or below); drop it in the empty space at the foot of the list \u2014 or use its \u22ee "Move to top level" \u2014 to pull it out to the base level, in no project. Tapping an open project again now closes it.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 the task log is now a clean thread of cards. Each entry is one tidy row (comment, attach, reply, menu); Add entry opens a card with title, comment and attachments; one Attach menu adds a file OR a wireframe / sheet / doc / sketch; Reply threads under an entry; and attachments open full screen. Tasks hold sub-tasks (a tree in the middle column); comments hold their own replies and attachments.\n\u2022 Applet store \u2014 you can now sort by newly released, recently published or recently updated, and filter each date to a time window (past day, week, month or year) from the new chips under the search box. Each tile shows its release date and when it was last published and updated. Applets that don\'t carry a date sort to the end and drop out of time-window filters.\n\u2022 Terrain \u2014 a unified, reproducible observatory over the whole transit / urban model stack. One grouplet ties it together: a model + dataset CATALOG (what each model uses and produces, which cities and countries), an editable bublee LOGIC-CHAIN of the four-step pipeline (every step and every data arrow is a live row you can add, edit, or swap \u2014 even the middle), the analytics COCKPIT (the report dashboard engine \u2014 view, compare, %-delta, slices), sliceable metric families (quality of life, traffic, economic, health, jobs, food\u2026), a run LEDGER, and \u2014 the spine \u2014 a REPRODUCIBILITY trace built for government-grant review: each run pins a model version, its input datasets with provenance, the 11-check soundness audit (FHWA / NCHRP-716 / TMIP / STOPS) it passed, and a confidence tier. Atlas folds in unchanged as the live layered map. Compute stays server-side; this build shows precomputed + importable rows honestly.\n\u2022 Model reports \u2014 the full analytics build-out. Tap a bar on the dashboard to select that category and watch every other view filter to it (tap again to clear); tap a legend entry to hide a series. A new Metrics tab keeps derived numbers (totals, retention %) cached as rows \u2014 recalculate on demand instead of recomputing every view. The Map is now a true ADD-ON: districts shade by cached totals, tapping a district filters everything to it, a chip hides the overlay \u2014 and the whole screen can be switched off under \u22ee \u2192 Add-ons. That same Add-ons switch is the socket future modules (a stock-market watch, a many-axis explorer) plug into.\n\u2022 Hexnom / Nomnoms \u2014 the defend-the-settlement game grew up. Buildings are SOLID now: enemies walk around them, and chew on whatever they brush past (a red bar shows the damage) \u2014 so a wall up front really protects the farms behind it. Your little folk push apart instead of stacking, and circle a building while they work it. NEW in the \ud83d\udc65 panel: PROTOCOLS \u2014 standing orders you predefine and can change mid-wave: keep food above a floor you set, keep wood stocked, add houses when crowded, stand up the tower YOU pick (archer, goo or sunray) before each wave, and rebuild whatever falls. Plus a production QUEUE the base works through in order, and \ud83d\udcbe save \u2014 park a run any time, resume from the menu.\n\u2022 NEW applet: Model reports \u2014 a report dashboard for comparing model outputs (scenario runs, forecasts, any table of results). Chart or table views with filters, sorts, group-by rollups (sum/avg/median/count), a percent-difference compare against a base run, and saved views you can reload in one tap. Import your own CSV on the Data tab (the seeded rows are just a fictional demo of the shape); an optional Map tab plots rows with coordinates and can simply be removed if you don\'t need it.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 undo works properly now: one move, drag or resize undoes in a single step, and Ctrl/Cmd+Z (plus Shift+Z or Ctrl+Y to redo) work on the canvas as well as the toolbar arrows. You can grab a covered block by its floating name label and drag the whole thing (and anything inside it). And right-click a block for stacking order \u2014 bring to front / forward / backward / send to back \u2014 plus edit and delete.\n\u2022 Web \u2014 updates are smoother now. A new build no longer gets stuck reloading in a loop, and no version code lingers in the address bar. There\'s also a "Hard update" option in the menu that force-refreshes onto the freshest build \u2014 and it ONLY clears the app cache, so your applet data and sign-in are always kept.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 panning a wireframe no longer scrolls the whole page with it; the canvas now owns the trackpad pan. Plus full-screen wireframe/docs open as a single isolated canvas (no applet file tree), and entries hold a list of attachments you add.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 a comment shows its TITLE at the top no matter the content (note, sheet, doc or wireframe); the Type dropdown now rebuilds the content immediately; and scrolling inside an embedded widget stays inside it instead of dragging the whole thread.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 an embedded sheet, canvas or note inside a comment now renders on a clean white surface instead of a dimmed/tinted panel.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 the thread is now ONE smooth scroll. Open a task and the whole right side scrolls together: the task details at the top, then its conversation, then an "Add to thread" card at the very bottom \u2014 tap it to add a Comment, Wireframe, Sheet, Doc, or Sketch. No floating button, no separate pinned header.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 the task details at the top now show just the essentials (title, status, details), with a clear "Edit details" button to change them in place.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 edit any item right where it sits, no popup. Tap a card to open it read-only; the pencil (top-right) turns editing on, the check finishes, the X cancels and undoes your changes. A "Type" dropdown switches the item between Note, Doc, Sheet, Wireframe and Sketch; the \u22ee menu adds a subtask or deletes; and the expand icon opens it full-screen. Subtasks show nested under their parent.\n\u2022 Notes \u2014 view, edit, and choose how it saves. A note has Write and Preview (edit vs read), and in Write mode you now pick how edits commit: "Auto" saves continuously as you type (the default), or "Manual" holds your edits until you tap Save. So a log\'s note can be live-updating or save-on-demand, your call. (Used by Dispatch logs and anywhere notes appear.)\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 every thread item is a "log" you open into its own view. Tap a card (or add one) and it opens full-screen: a NOTE by default \u2014 an optional title plus the text \u2014 and a "Change content" menu (top right) that swaps the note for an embedded Wireframe, Spreadsheet, Document, or Sketch, kept right there with the item. So a comment is just a note, and any item can become a suite tool whenever you want.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 the task you open now has a header. The right pane shows the task\'s details (title, status, and a description) at the top, above its thread \u2014 and a "Settings" button there opens the full task editor so you can update those details. So it reads top-to-bottom: the task, then its conversation.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 one "+ Add to thread" for everything. The task thread is a single full-width column of cards now (no more side-by-side grid, no inline comment line). Tap "+ Add to thread" and pick what to drop in: a Comment, or an embedded suite tool \u2014 Wireframe, Notes, Sheet, Doc, or Sketch \u2014 each living as its own item in the thread with its own canvas, kept with the task and signed by whoever added it.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 the tasks (middle panel) are pretty cards now. Each task in the picked project shows as its own rounded card with its status, and the one you\'re viewing is highlighted \u2014 easier to scan than a plain list.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 add a comment with a "+" that opens a fill-in form. Under a task, "+ New comment (with details)" opens a popup where you set the title, a longer description, and who it\'s prompting \u2014 for a considered reply, not just a one-liner. (The quick "Write a comment\u2026" box is still there for fast notes.)\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 a proper 3-pane board for working together. Left: your projects. Middle: the tasks in the picked project (each with a status). Right: that task\'s comment thread \u2014 cards stacked newest-last, with a box at the bottom to add one (signed with your name) and an "Add a wireframe" button. Pick a project, pick a task, talk it through \u2014 and others can add comments and wireframes to the same task, live.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 a comment can be a wireframe, right in the thread. Tap "Add a wireframe" under a task and it drops a wireframe card into the feed; tap that card to open its own canvas full-screen, sketch, and come back \u2014 each one keeps its own drawing. (Plain comments still open their text editor on tap.)\n\u2022 Back up EVERYTHING in one tap \u2014 the main menu now has "Back up everything", which downloads all your applets and their data (your journal included) as a single file you keep. Pairs with each applet\'s own "Back up to file" and is independent of any server backup \u2014 your safety copy, on your device.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 comments are now a real thread. Open a task and its comments stack as a FEED of cards, one building on the next, with a box at the bottom to add a new one (signed with your name). Tap a card to edit it. Before, clicking a comment took over the whole pane and showed just that one \u2014 now you see the whole conversation at a glance.\n\u2022 Back up any applet to a file \u2014 each applet\'s \u22ee Data menu now has "Back up to file\u2026" (downloads the applet + its data as a file you keep) and "Restore from file\u2026". A safety copy that lives on YOUR device, separate from save points and from any whole-app backup. Works in the web app now too.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 share a board and work it together, with names on every comment. On the People tab, tap "This is me" to set your display name, then "Share this board" to invite another profile by code. When two people are in, the thread updates live (about a second), and each comment is signed with whoever wrote it \u2014 so you can see who said what. (Sharing is by code for now; a proper "remove access" comes later.)\n\u2022 Staying up to date \u2014 Settings now opens with a "Version & updates" card showing your build and WHEN it was last updated, plus an "Update now" button right there. The web app also refreshes itself onto the newest version automatically when you open it (no more clearing your browser to see changes); turn that off any time with the "Auto-update to latest" switch.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 see and set a task\'s status, plus a tidier collapse. Each task now shows a STATUS chip (active / queued / done) right in the list, and its \u22ee menu sets the status in one tap \u2014 so a shared work board reads at a glance and you can mark progress without opening anything. And collapsing a side panel now tucks it into a slim strip on the EDGE, instead of stacking two bars across the top.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 full selection toolkit: pick the Select tool and choose how to select \u2014 Box, Lasso (draw freehand), Poly (tap points), or Wand (tap to grab a whole same-colour area). A selection now CONSTRAINS what you do: draw, erase, bucket-fill and gradients only land inside it, and you can drag the selected pixels to MOVE them.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 layer masks: hide or reveal parts of a layer without erasing anything. Make a selection, then "Mask from selection" on the layer \u2014 what is outside is hidden but never lost (clear the mask to bring it back).\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 more layer tools + a pixel mode: merge a layer down onto the one below, and a new "Pixel" toggle for hard, crisp pixel-art edges (off = smooth, the default).\n\u2022 Architecture app refreshed: two new tabs \u2014 "Claude workflow" (how this app gets built, step by step) and "What\'s new" (everything shipped recently, by area).\n\u2022 3D layer view \u2014 now shows the REAL components and you can spin it. The "3D layers" button used to draw every block as the same grey tile; it now tilts your ACTUAL blocks (a button looks like a button, a list like rows) at their true size and position. Drag on the view to rotate it, or tap the rotate button (bottom-left) to cycle a few angles, and it eases in AND out smoothly instead of snapping.\n\u2022 Canvas \u2014 a tap-to-reveal label for hidden blocks. When a block is mostly covered by another on top of it, a small chip now appears naming the hidden block \u2014 tap it to select that block and bring it to the front. It only shows when something is actually hidden, so no clutter.\n\u2022 Export \u2014 your drawn marks come along now. Exporting a wireframe to an image (SVG) or to draw.io used to drop the freehand "Sketch on top" lines and text; now they are included in the file, so the export matches what you see (and the PNG). (Still to come: matching the exact block colours and arrow routing in those two formats.)\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 pivot tables + export to open formats. Right-click a selection \u2192 "Pivot table\u2026", choose what goes in rows and columns and which value to summarise (sum, count, average, min or max) \u2014 you get a live cross-tab with totals you can export. "Export\u2026" now offers CSV, TSV and Markdown (open, non-proprietary formats); import reads CSV or TSV. No Excel files yet.\n\u2022 3D layer view \u2014 now true to your layout. The "3D layers" button (tilt the canvas to see how blocks stack) used to fan everything out and draw it all as the same grey tile, so sizes, positions and colours were off. It is now a faithful tilt: each block keeps its real SIZE and its real position relative to the others (it just leans back and lifts apart by layer so you can see what is underneath), and every block shows in ITS OWN colour instead of flat grey. (Next for 3D: real component shapes, animated transitions, and more viewing angles.)\n\u2022 Wireframe & design \u2014 "Bring forward / Send back" now actually re-stacks blocks. Select a block (or a few) and use the layers menu in the selection toolbar: Bring to front / forward / Send backward / to back. It used to change the order under the hood but you could not SEE it \u2014 now the block visibly moves above or below the ones it overlaps (and is grabbed in that order too), so when a section hides another you can lift the one you want on top. Blocks that share an order still fall back to size \u2014 big frames sit behind their smaller contents \u2014 so nothing changes until you ask it to. Now switched on in the Design and Maker canvases as well as Wireframe.\n\u2022 Dispatch \u2014 a cleaner work board. Projects open with their tasks already listed, the left panel collapses, and the nested tiers tuck away neatly. A task opens its updates on the RIGHT as a side panel (no pop-up). An update reads as a clean card by default \u2014 tap Edit (the pencil) to change it, then the check \u2713 saves it live and drops back to read-view, so a posted note isn\'t rewritten by accident.\n\u2022 Wireframe & diagrams \u2014 your drawn marks are real objects now. A line, shape or text label you draw on top of a diagram (the "Sketch on top" pencil) used to be stuck where you made it. Now TAP one to select it, DRAG to move it (one undo puts it back), and grab a corner handle to RESIZE it \u2014 it scales the drawing but keeps the line exactly as thick as you drew it. Remove one with the Delete key or by dropping it on the trash bin. And selecting a drawn mark, a block, or a frame are cleanly separate now \u2014 picking one clears the others (a box-select over empty space deselects).\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 data validation (dropdown lists AND rules). Right-click a column header \u2192 "Data validation\u2026" and pick a rule: a dropdown LIST of allowed values (cells become a pick-list), or a constraint \u2014 number range, max text length, or date range. Anything that breaks the rule is kept but flagged in red (a warning, not a block); "None" clears it back to free text.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 type a function and it suggests the rest. Start typing "=SU" in the formula bar or a cell and a dropdown lists matching functions (SUM, SUMIF\u2026) with their arguments and a one-line description; \u2191\u2193 to pick, Tab or Enter to drop in "=SUM(" with the cursor ready inside. Esc dismisses.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 a built-in function guide. Tap the \u0192 icon next to the formula bar for a searchable list of every function \u2014 name, what goes in the brackets, and what it does \u2014 so you don\'t have to remember them.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 a big library of date, text and number functions for real data work. Dates: TODAY, NOW, DATE, YEAR/MONTH/DAY, WEEKDAY, EDATE, EOMONTH, DATEDIF (days/months/years between two dates). Text & numbers: VALUE turns text like "1,234" or "12%" into a number, TEXT/FIXED/DOLLAR format a number as text, CONCAT/LEFT/RIGHT/MID build strings. Plus ROW() \u2014 put "=ROW()-1" down a column to auto-number 1, 2, 3\u2026 \u2014 and CHOOSE / SWITCH / MROUND.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 fill a series and add rows in bulk. Select a couple of cells and "Fill series down/right" (right-click menu) continues the pattern \u2014 1, 2, 3\u2026 or one date per day \u2014 instead of just copying. And "Add 10/100 rows" adds many blank rows at once.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 colour-scale highlighting + a live selection summary. Right-click a column header \u2192 "Color scale" tints its cells red\u2192yellow\u2192green by value, so highs and lows jump out. And selecting a range of numbers shows a Count / Sum / Average / Min / Max strip along the bottom.\n\u2022 Friendlier empty screens, app-wide. When a list has nothing in it yet -- no notes, no tasks, an empty pantry -- most apps now show a helpful little panel (an icon, a line like "No notes yet -- create your first note", and an Add button where it makes sense) instead of a blank area. Apps that pull live or seeded data (like crypto prices or the bike-share map) get a fitting "how this fills up" hint instead.\n\u2022 Tabletop (NEW) \u2014 a free-form shared card table with NO built-in rules: drag a card anywhere, tap to flip, long-press to rotate it 90\xb0, flick it across the felt, and stack the deck. Zoom with the +/- buttons. Deal from the deck however you like and make up your own game; share by code to play live with others.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 LIVE collaboration (foundation). Sign in, open the \u22ee menu \u2192 "Share by code", and another person who joins with that code now edits the SAME wireframe with you \u2014 moves, new blocks and edits show up live (about a second), merged per-field so you don\'t clobber each other. (Coming next: seeing who changed what, a change history, and open/no-account sharing.)\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 keyboard Delete/Backspace now works after a drag-select. Marquee-drag to select a group of blocks, then press Delete or Backspace to remove them \u2014 the keyboard shortcut now fires immediately after any selection method (drag, long-press, or tap). Previously it only worked reliably after tapping a block.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 deleting several blocks asks first, and you can confirm with the keyboard. Select multiple blocks and hit Delete (button or key) \u2192 a "Delete?" prompt appears (so a big delete is never an accident). Delete is pre-selected, so just press ENTER to confirm; press \u2190 (arrow) to move to Cancel, or Esc, to back out. Deleting a single block is unchanged.\n\u2022 Hide the side list for more room. In Notes, Docs and the Wireframe Pages panel, the left list now has a collapse arrow \u2014 tap it to tuck the list away and give the page/note/canvas the full width, and a button to bring it back. Great on a narrow browser window or phone where the list and the content were squeezing each other. (Sketchpad already worked this way.)\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 a 3D view of your layers. Tap the new "3D" button (cube icon, in the zoom rail) and the canvas tilts into an isometric view that lifts each block apart by its stacking order, with shadows \u2014 so you can SEE how things stack (a nav bar floating above a screen). It animates in; tap it off to go back to editing.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 see what\'s underneath. A new "labels" button (in the right-side zoom rail) floats each block\'s name as a small tag ABOVE it \u2014 so when a nav bar sits on top of a screen, you still see BOTH names. When blocks stack, the tags fan upward so none hides behind another. Tap it off anytime.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 double-tap a block to rename its text. Quickly give any block the words you want \u2014 a "section" that reads "Response", an input that reads "Email" or "Phone" \u2014 without opening the full editor. Double-tap \u2192 type \u2192 Enter. (A single tap still just selects, instantly.)\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 deleting several blocks is now ONE undo. Select a bunch of blocks (and their arrows) and delete them, and a single Undo brings the whole lot back \u2014 it used to take one undo per block. And when a delete asks "Delete?", you can just press ENTER to confirm (no reaching for the mouse).\n\u2022 Live rooms use less battery. When you\'re in a shared room (the games, and soon live collaboration), the app gets changes over a single live stream \u2014 so while that stream is connected it no longer ALSO polls the server every couple of seconds. Same instant updates, less drain; the poll stays only as a fallback if the stream drops.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 remove a saved widget. Each chip in the "Saved" tab now has a small \xd7 \u2014 tap it to take that widget out of your library (everywhere, not just this project), with an Undo if you change your mind. Drag still places it; the \xd7 only removes it.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 your saved widgets now FOLLOW YOU into every project. Bookmark a widget in one project and it shows up in the "Saved" tab of every other wireframe project too, marked with a \ud83c\udf10 badge so you know it\'s from your device-wide library. (Widgets you saved in the current project still show there as before; the library is in addition.) They\'re stored on your device, so they don\'t get carried into a project file you share.\n\u2022 Undo/redo works everywhere now. The top-bar Undo/Redo buttons already covered every app; now \u2318Z / Ctrl+Z (and \u2318\u21e7Z / \u2318Y to redo) work from the keyboard in EVERY applet too, not just Sheets \u2014 over the same data history.\n\u2022 Your launcher setup follows you across devices now. When you\'re signed in, your FAVORITES (plus your groups, app renames and collapsed sections) save to your account \u2014 so a new phone or browser shows the same setup instead of making you re-favorite everything. It still works offline (saved on the device too) and updates when you come back online. (Signed out, it stays on that one device, same as before.)\n\u2022 Podcasts \u2014 a "Discover" tab that recommends new shows to follow. Tap "Find shows for you" and it suggests podcasts you don\'t follow yet that bridge the interests of the shows you DO follow (e.g. "Connects Tech and Story"), using the same on-device, no-key bridge finder as Watchlist.\n\u2022 Watchlist \u2014 a "Bridges" recommender that helps you discover ACROSS your interests. Tap "Find my bridges" and it surfaces the unwatched videos that connect your different topics (the ones linking, say, your science and tech sides), each with a reason like "Connects Science and Tech". It is computed entirely on your device from how your videos connect \u2014 no account, no API key, nothing leaves your phone.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 export your design as a PNG image. The camera button in the canvas controls (bottom-right) saves a picture of the canvas \u2014 it downloads in the browser and shares as a file on a phone. (SVG export gives you a crisp scalable version; PNG is a quick flat snapshot of the current view.)\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 undo now brings deleted rows back for good. Deleting rows then undoing used to leave them gone (on a synced device the deletion quietly won again); now an undo re-instates the rows with a fresh stamp that wins everywhere, and redo removes them again. Applies to every undo/redo.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 clear cells with the toolbar eraser (no Delete key needed). The Clear button (eraser icon) in the toolbar \u2014 and "Clear contents" in the long-press menu \u2014 wipe the selected cell(s) on any device. Select one cell or many, then clear.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 style a block\'s text. Select one or more blocks and the new Text button (the A icon) in the selection toolbar sets the label\'s size (Small / Medium / Large), alignment (left / center / right) and Bold. It layers on top of the themed look \u2014 only what you change changes, everything else keeps its normal style.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 saving a widget you already saved no longer makes a duplicate. If you bookmark a group whose exact structure (the same blocks and arrows) is already in your Saved tab, it updates that one and floats it to the TOP of the tab instead of stacking a second copy \u2014 even if you give it a new name. Your saved widgets also now list newest-first.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 more shapes + a search box. The widgets drawer gained a dozen more shapes (Heading, Paragraph, Checkbox, Dropdown, Search bar, Tabs, Card, Avatar, Icon, Divider, Table, Footer) on top of the basics. With that many, a Search box now sits at the top of the drawer \u2014 type to filter the shapes by name so you can grab the one you want fast.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 LAYERS. A page can now hold layers \u2014 sheets of blocks you can show/hide and lock independently (e.g. a background layer under a labels layer). The drawer has a new Layers panel: tap "New layer", then select blocks and use the layers button (\u25a6) in the selection toolbar to move them onto it. Toggle a layer\'s eye to hide its blocks, or its lock to pin them. Blocks not on any layer always show, as before.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 select several cells on a phone (no Ctrl key needed). Long-press a cell \u2192 "Select multiple"; now every tap ADDS that cell to the selection (scattered cells, not just a block). A bar shows how many are selected with Clear and Done buttons \u2014 tap Clear to wipe them all, or Done to finish. (On desktop, \u2318/Ctrl-click still does the same.)\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 your saved widgets now have their OWN tab. The drawer shows a "Widgets" tab (the basic blocks) and a "Saved" tab (the custom widgets you bookmark), so your own building blocks stay together and out of the way of the basics. The Saved tab also tells you how to make one when it\'s empty.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 your drawer order sticks now. If you drag the widget chips into a different order, that order is remembered the next time you open the app (it used to reset to the default on every restart).\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 the drawer gets out of your way while you place a widget. On a phone, when you drag a widget out of the drawer, the drawer now slides aside so the whole board is a drop zone \u2014 then slides back when you let go. The widget stays held the whole time (the drag is never dropped), so placing a block onto the canvas is no longer a fight with the panel.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 export your design as an IMAGE. A new "Export image (SVG)" button in the page drawer saves the open page as an SVG picture \u2014 it downloads in the browser and shares as a file on a phone, and opens anywhere (it\'s a crisp, scalable vector image of your boxes and arrows).\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 the formula bar now works properly on phone/browser. Backspace edits the formula bar (it was wrongly clearing the cell instead), and pressing return/Enter commits your formula (e.g. =F4+F5) instead of losing it. To clear one or more cells, the eraser button (Clear contents) in the toolbar \u2014 or "Clear contents" in the long-press menu \u2014 wipes every selected cell at once.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 real connectors. Arrows now actually look like arrows \u2014 every connector gets an ARROWHEAD at its end. Select a connector and the new Connector-style menu (the \u223f icon) lets you pick its shape \u2014 Straight, Elbow (right-angle) or Curved \u2014 turn the arrowhead on/off, and switch the line to Solid, Dashed or Dotted. Any label on the link shows at the middle of the line.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 SAVE YOUR OWN WIDGETS. Select a group of blocks (a card with an input and a button, a header, whatever you build a lot), tap the new bookmark button in the selection toolbar, give it a name \u2014 and it joins a "Your widgets" section at the bottom of the drawer. Drag it back onto the board to drop the whole group at once, blocks and the arrows between them included. Your saved widgets travel inside the project file, so a shared project keeps them.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 a tidier block editor. Tapping a block to edit its details now shows just the things you actually set \u2014 Label, Kind and Note \u2014 instead of a long form full of internal numbers (position, size, z-order, lock, colours). Those are still set by dragging, resizing and the toolbar; a "show hidden fields" link reveals them if you ever need to. And Kind is now a dropdown (frame / nav / button / input / text / image / list / section), so you pick the block type instead of typing it.\n\u2022 Two new Media apps on your shelf \u2014 Podcasts and Watchlist. Podcasts: follow shows, queue + rate episodes, track what you have listened to. Watchlist: save video/YouTube recommendations with why they were recommended, sort by channel/topic/priority, mark watched + rate. Both open links in your real apps; live podcast search and YouTube recs are next.\n\u2022 Both games run themselves now \u2014 and it is finally watchable. A big autoplay upgrade across Noms and Hexnom: a SPEED dial (1\xd7 / 2\xd7 / 4\xd7 / 8\xd7) to fast-forward the action; a \ud83d\udcac "what is happening" feed that narrates every move the auto-player makes and why ("\ud83e\udd16 digging a water channel so trees can grow", "\ud83c\udfd7\ufe0f building a market by the trees"); a smarter auto-player that builds toward a thriving, connected world instead of wandering; and a one-tap "Full auto-run" that switches it all on (auto + narration + fast speed) so you can sit back and watch a society grow or a city build itself.\n\u2022 Hexnom \u2014 zoom OUT to a neighbourhood map: pull back and the city becomes districts, each a node showing its money rising \u25b2 or falling \u25bc and the traffic between them; zoom in for the detailed streets. Plus colony and city balance fixes so worlds actually grow, clearer warnings, and tidier controls.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 the widgets drawer now PREVIEWS each widget. Every chip shows a tiny picture of what it actually looks like \u2014 a button looks like a button, an input like a field, a list like rows \u2014 instead of a generic icon, so you can pick the right block at a glance.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 pasted cells now FLASH for a moment so you can see exactly what landed (same for an imported CSV).\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 zoom works with the Mac trackpad. Pinch to zoom the grid, or use \u2318+ / \u2318- (and \u23180 to reset to 100%); Ctrl/\u2318 + scroll zooms too. Two-finger pinch on cells used to do nothing \u2014 fixed.\n\u2022 Web \u2014 a two-finger swipe no longer accidentally navigates the browser back/forward a page; the app now contains the gesture (cleanly, without the old janky history trap).\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 a widget that doesn\'t land springs back. If you drag a widget out of the drawer but let go over empty space (or somewhere it can\'t drop), it now ANIMATES back to the drawer instead of just vanishing \u2014 a clear "that didn\'t place" cue, so a missed drop never feels like you lost the widget. (This is a general touch across the app: drags that fail now visibly revert.)\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 formulas are easier to read and build (like Excel/Sheets). While you edit a formula, each cell it references is shown in its own COLOR \u2014 the reference text and the matching cell on the grid light up the same color, so =SUM(G5,F5,H5) clearly shows its three cells. Typing "(" auto-adds the closing ")". And you can POINT with the arrow keys: after "=" or an operator, press an arrow and a selector moves to the cell (Down = the cell below) and drops its reference in \u2014 keep arrowing to move it.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 reverse an arrow. Select a connector and tap the new Reverse button (the \u21c4 icon) to flip its direction so the arrowhead points the other way \u2014 a connector has no rotate/flip, reversing direction IS its flip; the glued ends stay glued to their sides.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 smoother zoom, cleaner connections, easier selecting. Pinch to ZOOM with a trackpad in the browser now works (it used to do nothing). Connecting two blocks now draws the line CENTRE-TO-CENTRE by default, so arrows look tidy automatically \u2014 you only pin it to a specific side or corner if you drop the link there on purpose. And on a Mac, dragging a selection box no longer makes the browser try to highlight the page \u2014 a normal click-drag draws the selection rectangle (no hard press needed).\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 building a formula by clicking cells now ADDS them up. Type "=", click a cell, type "+" (or any operator), click another cell \u2192 you get =A1+B1 instead of it resetting to just the last cell. Works in the cell and in the formula bar. (Copy/paste a formula already shifts its references to the new position.)\n\u2022 Editing \u2014 the record editor now opens FULL-SCREEN. Tapping a row to edit (or adding one) used to slide up a half-height sheet, or split off a side panel on wide screens. Now it fills the screen on phone, tablet and browser \u2014 no more cramped \u201chalf and half\u201d. (Drag the grab handle down to close.)\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 drag a box to select, and richer copy. Drag on empty canvas to draw a SELECTION RECTANGLE \u2014 it selects every block it touches, on phone and desktop (two fingers still pan). On desktop, Ctrl/Cmd-click a block to add it to \u2014 or remove it from \u2014 the selection. And Copy now brings along a block\'s contents and the arrows between copied blocks, so copying a whole screen reproduces its wiring. (On a phone, the hold-to-drag from the widgets drawer is snappier now, with a little buzz when a widget lifts.)\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 lock blocks. Select a block (or several) and tap the lock icon in the selection toolbar to pin it: a locked block can\'t be moved or resized, so you can build on top of a background without nudging it. Tap the lock again to unlock.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 import and export CSV. Right-click a cell \u2192 "Export CSV" saves the whole sheet as a .csv file (downloads on web, shares on phone), and "Import CSV\u2026" opens a paste box \u2014 drop in comma- or tab-separated data and it fills the grid from the top-left, adding rows and columns as needed.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 standard special characters. Start a cell with an apostrophe to force plain TEXT (\'123 stays "123", \'=A1 shows the text instead of calculating), write percentages directly (50% = 0.5, 200*15% = 30), and join text with & (=A1&"!").\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 align, distribute & order (like a real design tool). Select two or more blocks, then use the Align button in the selection toolbar to align left/center/right/top/middle/bottom, distribute them evenly, or make them the same size. The Order button sends a block to the front or back so overlapping blocks stack the way you want.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 keyboard shortcuts (desktop/web). Click the canvas, then: Delete or Backspace removes the selected block(s); Esc clears the selection; Cmd/Ctrl+D duplicates; Cmd/Ctrl+A selects everything; and the arrow keys nudge the selection (hold Shift to move by a whole grid step).\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 clearer selecting + a copy button + named looks. Tapping a block now selects just that ONE (tap another to switch). To select several, press-and-HOLD a block to start multi-select, then tap more to add or remove them. The pop-up menu is gone \u2014 Copy and Delete are now icons in the selection toolbar (Copy duplicates every selected block, keeping its kind, size and colour). And the look button is clearer: TAP it to cycle Design \u2192 Wireframe \u2192 Detail, or HOLD it to pick the look from a short list.\n\u2022 Nav button \u2014 place it ANYWHERE now. The floating tabs button is no longer limited to a few fixed spots: press and hold, drag it wherever you like, and it STAYS there \u2014 it only snaps to a corner or edge when you let go right near one. (Its "Button settings" gear still has quick-spot presets and "Hide tab name".)\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 block styling now actually shows. The Style button (select a few blocks \u2192 palette icon) sets fill colour, border colour and thickness across them all \u2014 and the fill is now the BLOCK\u2019S colour in the themed wireframe look (it used to be hidden behind the block). Pick a colour and the button / section / nav actually turns that colour.\n\u2022 Maker \u2014 the builder grew up: the new Logic tab wires a screen element to a real backend action (adds a row, writes a field\u2026); the Data tab now gives each table TYPED fields (text, number, date, yes/no, a choice list, or a link to another table); Build app from wireframe turns all of that into a real working app \u2014 typed tables and a button that actually adds a row, not an empty shell. Fill example data and the new Draft with the model button use your on-device model (reading your own design) and fall back to handy examples when no model is running. Pickers now show friendly names (e.g. "habit") instead of internal ids.\n\u2022 The web app updates itself now. The browser version used to get "stuck" on an old copy after an update went out, so new features looked missing until you cleared your browser. Now, when a newer version is live, a little "A new version is ready \u2014 Reload" bar appears; tap Reload and it drops the old cached copy and loads the fresh one (it also re-checks when you come back to the tab). No more clearing site data by hand.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 export a single page as a file too: a page\'s drawer now has "Export this page (.isan)" alongside the draw.io buttons. It saves just the open page as a lossless .isan file you can drop into another project (Import project adds its page). So you can move a whole project OR one screen between projects.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 symmetry drawing: flip on the new Symmetry toggle and every stroke is mirrored across the middle of the canvas as you draw \u2014 great for faces, patterns and anything that should be balanced left-to-right. A faint guide line shows the mirror axis.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 a big library of formulas for real data work. On top of SUM/AVERAGE/IF you now have: stats (MEDIAN, STDEV, VAR, PERCENTILE, MODE, CORREL, SLOPE), lookups (VLOOKUP, INDEX, MATCH), conditional totals (COUNTIF, SUMIF, AVERAGEIF), logic (AND, OR, NOT, IFERROR, IFS), text (LEFT, RIGHT, MID, LEN, TRIM, UPPER, LOWER, CONCAT, SUBSTITUTE, FIND) and math (ROUND, ABS, SQRT, POWER, LOG, EXP, MOD). About 40 functions in all.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 typing "=" then clicking a cell now fills the reference into the formula from the FORMULA BAR too, not just when editing inside the cell. Click or drag a range and it drops in as A1 or A1:B3.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 more keyboard shortcuts: \u2318X cut, \u2318B bold, \u2318I italic, and \u2318+arrow to jump to the edge of your data (\u2318\u21e7+arrow to select to it).\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 make a chart from your data. Select a range, right-click (or long-press) \u2192 "Create chart\u2026", then pick Bar or Scatter and which columns to use; a live preview shows as you choose.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 align cells left, center or right. Three alignment buttons in the format bar set how text and numbers sit in the cell; tap the active one again to clear it. Numbers still line up on the right by default, but now you can override that anywhere.\n\u2022 Exports download for real on the web now: "Export project (.isan)" and "Export to draw.io" save an actual file to your downloads instead of showing text to copy. (On a phone they still share the file through the system sheet.)\n\u2022 Both games leveled up (their engine got a big upgrade).\n  Noms \u2014 cultures now MEET at their borders and either CLASH or BLEND by how alike they are: close cultures melt together, distant ones bicker (turn on the \ud83c\udfad culture view to see the seams; the HUD shows a \u26a1 clash count). Each culture also grows a TRAIT \u2014 \ud83c\udf81 Rite of Sharing (generous) or \ud83d\udeab Taboo of Hoarding (frugal, resists going decadent) \u2014 and it changes how they behave, so a communal culture shares more and cooperates harder under the same policies. Tap any nom to INSPECT it (energy, happiness, indulgence, culture + trait, tech, zone), and tap the ? for a LEGEND of the ring colours. Plus fixes: share a SEED for the exact same world again, Grow zones really grow trees now, a decadent society can RECOVER when you raise self-control, and big maps pan all the way to the edges.\n  Hexnom \u2014 PASSENGERS arrived: build a \ud83d\ude89 Station to gather travel demand from nearby noms, and \ud83d\ude8c buses (and cars/trains) board them and pay \ud83c\udfab fares into the city when they arrive \u2014 people move value just like freight, only as fast as the network carries them. New \ud83d\udcc8 BROKERAGE: invest spare treasury into a market for ongoing dividends that grow it (a growth lever, distinct from a loan, which costs interest). The \ud83e\udd16 auto-mayor is smarter \u2014 it spreads buildings out, upgrades jammed roads, builds stations and runs buses, and invests when flush. And the city reads clearer: \ud83d\udd0c marks a building with no road to value, a coach names your top blocker, \ud83d\udea6 shows traffic jams, and the bank now shows interest per second (it was mislabeled \u2014 a finance-spiral fix).\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 duplicate & flatten layers: each layer in the Layers panel now has a Duplicate button (copies it, marks, blend mode and all), and a Flatten button merges everything you see into one layer when you are ready to bake it down.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 save your whole project as a file: the Project tab has "Export project (.isan)" and "Import project (.isan)". The .isan file is lossless \u2014 every page and folder, with its blocks, arrows and sketches \u2014 so you can back up, move, or share a whole project and bring it back exactly (folders, page tags and arrows all reconnect). draw.io export/import (in a page\'s drawer) still handles a single page for working with other tools.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 find & replace, like every spreadsheet. Tap the find-and-replace button in the toolbar (or press \u2318F / Ctrl+F) to open a search bar: type what to look for and it shows "1 of N", with up/down arrows to jump between matches (each one is selected and scrolled into view). Type a replacement and "Replace" changes just the current match, or "All" changes every match at once \u2014 and a single Undo puts it all back. Esc closes the bar.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 brush presets: a one-tap brush menu in the tool bar with Pen, Pencil, Marker, Airbrush and Highlighter \u2014 each sets a familiar size, opacity and soft-edge so you do not have to dial in the sliders every time (you still can).\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 number formatting, like every spreadsheet. A new format-bar menu (the # button) sets a cell to Number (1,234.56), Currency ($1,234.56) or Percent (12%), and the +1 / -1 buttons add or remove decimal places. It only changes how the value LOOKS \u2014 the real number stays underneath, so formulas keep adding it up and the formula bar still shows what you typed. Formatted numbers line up on the right, like a spreadsheet.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 style several blocks at once. Tap a few blocks to select them, then tap the new "Style" button (palette icon) in the selection toolbar: pick a fill colour, a border colour, or a border thickness and it applies to ALL of them. When the selected blocks already differ on something it shows "Mixed", and setting a value makes them match \u2014 the way Figma and draw.io do it. (This first version covers fill, border colour and thickness; shape type and connection styling \u2014 arrows, dashed lines \u2014 are coming next.)\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 keyboard navigation, like every spreadsheet. \u2318A (Ctrl+A) selects the whole sheet; Home/End jump to the first/last column of the row; Page Up / Page Down move a screenful of rows at a time; \u2318Home / \u2318End jump to the top-left cell / the very last cell. Hold Shift with any of these to extend the selection instead of just moving, and the grid always scrolls the cell you land on into view.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 pinch to zoom: two fingers now zoom the grid in and out (50\u2013250%) as well as pan it \u2014 on a phone and on a trackpad. The zoom slider still works too.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 undo and redo: \u2318Z undoes your last change (\u2318\u21e7Z or \u2318Y redoes), and there are Undo/Redo buttons in the toolbar for phones (no keyboard needed). Covers edits, clears, deletes, paste, fill and formatting.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 multiple pages + folders: the Wireframe app now opens to a library of pages. Each page is its own canvas; tap one to open it, and only that page\'s blocks show (new blocks you add join the open page). Make a "New page" or a "New folder" to organize them \u2014 folders nest as deep as you like (a project is just a top folder), while a page stays a single canvas. Switch pages and the canvas swaps to that page. (Export/import and draw.io act on the open page; whole-project files are coming next.)\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 drag widgets out of the drawer (really fixed this time) + tap to multi-select. On a phone the drawer chip was caught between two hold-to-drag actions (drag-to-canvas vs reorder) so a hold did the wrong one; now it is ONE drag \u2014 drop it on the canvas to add a block, or on another chip to reorder. And you can TAP blocks to select several at once (tap toggles each in/out), then use the selection toolbar (top-right) to delete them all, or edit one when just one is selected.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 blocks drag ANYWHERE now, no invisible walls. Even after the last fix, there were edges: drag a block toward the far side of the canvas, or up/left past the starting corner, and it would show but refuse to be grabbed. The canvas is now truly endless \u2014 a block can be picked up and moved wherever it sits, however far out, in any direction.\n\u2022 Nav button \u2014 you can now move it and tweak it. The little floating button that opens your tabs (on phones with lots of tabs): press and HOLD it, then drag, and it goes where you drop it. Open its menu and tap the new gear ("Button settings") to pick its spot from a grid, or turn on "Hide tab name" so it shows just the page icon. Your choice is remembered. (Tap still opens the menu; a quick drag still flick-picks a tab.)\n\u2022 Maker & Wireframe \u2014 fresh, easy-to-drag example, plus a Reset button. The starter wireframe used to be one full-screen frame with every block packed inside it, which made the blocks awkward to grab on their own. It is now a clean, spread-out set of separate blocks (with one placed far out so you can see off-screen blocks drag too). And the app menu (\u22ee \u2192 "Reset to example data") now wipes your changes and reloads this clean starting set whenever you want \u2014 handy after experimenting.\n\u2022 New app \u2014 Wireframe (wireframing only): a dedicated wireframing app is now on your shelf, separate from the all-in-one Maker. It is just the canvas: a pull-out Widgets drawer (screens, nav, buttons, inputs, text, images, lists), the Connect / Draw / Text / Pan tools, snapping, and the draw.io Export / Import buttons. Opens on a starter screen so you can begin right away; "Build app from wireframe" turns it into a real app when you are ready.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 dragging blocks fixed (this is the big one). Two problems: blocks placed BEYOND the first screen looked there but would not move \u2014 the canvas now extends to wherever your blocks are, so every block can be grabbed no matter how far out it sits. And on a phone a stray touch the browser left behind could make one finger count as two, so a drag just panned instead of moving the block \u2014 now only fingers that actually MOVE count, so one finger reliably moves a block and two fingers pan/zoom.\n\u2022 Phone \u2014 the browser back-swipe works again. The app used to trap it (and the trap was both leaky and janky); that block is removed, so swiping from the screen edge goes back normally.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 works with draw.io: two new buttons in the Widgets drawer. "Export to draw.io" turns your screens and arrows into a .drawio file you can open and edit in diagrams.net (on a phone it shares the file; on the web it shows the XML to paste in). "Import from draw.io" reads a .drawio file back in \u2014 its boxes become blocks and its connectors become arrows, reconnected to the right blocks. (Use an UNCOMPRESSED .drawio / XML for import; draw.io can save one with compression off.)\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 blocks no longer get cut off: drag a screen far out to the edge and it stays whole instead of being cropped \u2014 the canvas is an endless surface, you just pan to follow it.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 drag widgets out of the drawer on a phone (fixed): the open drawer no longer blocks the drop, and it now takes up less of the screen so there is a clear strip of canvas to drop onto. Press and HOLD a widget, drag it onto the canvas, and let go. (A quick tap still drops one at a default spot, which you can then drag where you want.)\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 a Pan (hand) tool: tap the hand button in the canvas controls to switch to panning, then drag with ONE finger to move around the canvas (handy on a phone where two-finger panning is fiddly). Tap it again to go back to selecting and moving blocks.\n\u2022 Wireframe on a phone \u2014 less clutter: the little connect dot on every block is gone on phones (it was for drag-to-link with a mouse). Use the Connect tool to link blocks by tapping \u2014 and a one-finger drag never draws a stray selection rectangle on touch anymore.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 selecting and moving work again: a stray pointer the browser could leave behind was making the canvas think two fingers were down, so a one-finger drag did nothing but pan. Now only real finger touches count, so a one-finger drag selects (the box) and dragging a block moves it again \u2014 while two fingers still pan and pinch to zoom.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 zoom fixed: zooming in/out now keeps whatever is in the MIDDLE of the screen put (it used to jump everything to the top-left corner), and panning after you zoom OUT no longer races off at the wrong speed \u2014 it tracks your finger 1:1 at every zoom level. The reset button still snaps back to a clean 1:1 view.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 draw inside a selection: pick the Select tool, drag a box, and it now stays active as you keep working \u2014 anything you draw or erase is kept INSIDE that box, so you can fill or shade an area without spilling over the edges. Tap Deselect when you are done. (Copy region still works too.)\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 fill tools: an EYEDROPPER (tap to pick up any colour already on the canvas), a BUCKET fill (tap to flood a region with your colour), and a GRADIENT (drag to lay down a smooth colour fade). Find them in the tool row next to the pen.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 import & export, and flip the canvas: bring in a photo or image as its own layer, and export your work as a PNG, a transparent PNG, or an SVG (real vector lines you can scale forever) from the new "Export\u2026" menu. New buttons rotate, flip and mirror the whole drawing too.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 two-finger ZOOM fixed + an endless canvas: two fingers now pan AND pinch-to-zoom together (zoom used to get eaten by the pan). And a block dragged past the original edge is no longer cut off \u2014 the canvas just keeps going, so you can spread your screens out as far as you like.\n\u2022 New Transit group on the home screen, with two icons: "Transit forecasts" (a native applet) and "Transit dashboard" (opens the full web version, no sign-in). The applet has the same interactive analysis as the web \u2014 an Agent explorer and Cross-filter where you DRAG across distribution charts to slice a range, build two cohorts (A/B) to compare, and read a linked scatter \u2014 plus the forecasts and where-to-invest tables. On a phone the filter charts collapse: tap "Minimize filters" to give the results room.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 a Pan (hand) button: the canvas controls now include a hand icon next to zoom +/\u2212. Tap it on, then drag with one finger to move around the canvas \u2014 anywhere, even over a block; tap it off to go back to selecting. (Two fingers still pan and pinch still zooms either way.)\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 connectors are easier to work with: connector lines now draw ON TOP of your blocks (never hidden behind them), they are easier to tap-to-select, and the round endpoint handles have a bigger grab area \u2014 tap a connector, then drag one of its circles onto another block to re-route it.\n\u2022 Sheets on a phone \u2014 clearing and deleting made easy: a new eraser button in the toolbar clears the selected cell(s) with a tap (no keyboard needed), the red trash now deletes EVERY row you have selected (not just one), and the toolbar scrolls sideways so every button stays reachable on a small screen. (You can also long-press a cell for Clear contents / Delete rows.)\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 one consistent touch model on every device: a one-finger drag selects (a box), two fingers pan and pinch to zoom, and dragging a block moves it \u2014 the same on phone, tablet and computer. The moment a second finger lands it switches cleanly to pan/zoom (no stray selection box left behind).\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 connect screens by tapping: pick the new Connect tool (the arrow, top-left of the canvas next to Select/Draw/Text), tap one block, then tap another, and an arrow links them \u2014 no dragging needed, which is much easier on a phone. The chosen first block lights up while it waits for the second, and tapping it again (or tapping empty space) cancels. The arrow sticks to each block and follows them when you move or resize. (You can still drag from a block\'s connect dot if you prefer.)\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 pick any colour: tap the "+" beside the colour swatches to open a real colour picker \u2014 drag the shade square, slide the hue strip, or type a hex code (#33CC88). The colours you pick are kept as recent swatches so they are one tap away, instead of being stuck with the six presets.\n\u2022 Wireframe on a phone \u2014 touch now behaves: a one-finger drag MOVES the block under your finger (or draws / writes with the Draw and Text tools) instead of drawing a stray selection rectangle on the empty canvas; two fingers pan and pinch zooms. And to pull a widget out of the drawer, press and HOLD it, then drag onto the canvas (a quick tap still drops one at a default spot). On a computer a one-finger drag still box-selects, as before.\n\u2022 Calendar fills the screen: the month view now uses the full page height (taller day cells) instead of stopping halfway, so a whole month is easy to read. (This is an engine-wide "fill the space" fix \u2014 other single-view screens benefit too, while mixed screens keep scrolling as before.)\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 a real brush + an eraser: the pen now has an opacity slider (lay down translucent ink and build it up) and a soft-edge slider (from a crisp line to a soft airbrushed edge), and freehand lines are smoothed as you draw. There is also a proper eraser tool \u2014 it clears within the current layer, revealing whatever is on the layers below, so you can rub back into a drawing without wrecking it.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 see where a connector will attach: as you drag a connection from one block onto another, the target shows its anchor points (four corners + four side-midpoints) and lights up the one your arrow will snap to \u2014 so you can aim the connection exactly before you drop it.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 layer blend modes + opacity: every layer now has its own blend mode (multiply, screen, overlay, darken/lighten, dodge/burn, soft & hard light, and more) and an opacity slider, right in the Layers panel. Stack a multiply layer to deepen shadows, a screen layer to add glow, or drop a layer\'s opacity to ghost it back \u2014 the classic art-tool moves, with your drawing underneath left untouched.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 the grid follows you across the infinite canvas: the grid lines now fill the whole screen and move with the canvas as you pan and zoom, so they no longer stop at your blocks \u2014 there is always a grid to line up to, however far out you scroll.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 filter rows by column: tap the funnel on a column header to keep only the rows that match (contains/regex for text & numbers, a value checklist for choice/reference columns). Filtered columns show a solid funnel, and one button clears all filters. Sort, freeze, and =formulas all apply to the filtered view.\n\u2022 Maker \u2014 wire up behavior: a new Logic tab connects a screen element to a real action (adds a row, writes a field, filters a list\u2026) on one of your data tables \u2014 the companion to Bind, which says what an element shows. It comes with a live "Try it" demo (Add item really inserts a row; Fill example data drops a few in) and the wiring travels into the brief you hand to Claude when you build.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 change the grid size: a new ruler button in the canvas controls cycles the grid through 8 / 16 / 20 / 40 / 80 px. Everything follows it \u2014 snapping, where dropped blocks land, and the grid lines themselves \u2014 so you can work coarse for layout or fine for detail.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 an infinite canvas: pan as far as you like in any direction (two-finger drag, trackpad / wheel, or the controls) to spread your screens out with room to breathe \u2014 there is no edge to bump into now.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 pan and zoom on every device: two fingers pan and pinch zooms (or use the canvas controls / trackpad / wheel). On a computer a one-finger drag draws a selection box; on a phone a one-finger drag moves the block under it instead (see the phone touch fix above).\n\u2022 Maker Brief \u2014 one clear flow: your request sits at the top, and the features/specifics list is now clearly optional below it (instead of two equal halves splitting the screen).\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 freeze panes: row numbers and column letters now stay put as you scroll, and you can freeze the top rows / left columns too \u2014 select a cell and use the freeze button ("up to selection", or pick a number), so your headers and key columns stay on screen while you scroll a big sheet. "Unfreeze all" clears it.\n\u2022 Vertier \u2014 share a link to the community: tap "Share a link", paste a URL, and it\'s auto-titled and safety-checked ON YOUR DEVICE before posting (nothing about the link leaves your phone, no Google/third-party). Unsafe links (dangerous types, private/internal addresses, lookalike domains) are blocked or flagged; the feed shows each link as a card with its safety notice and warns you before opening anything unverified.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 see what moves together: when you select a frame (or drag it), the blocks sitting inside it get a soft highlight so you can tell at a glance which pieces will move with it when you drag the frame. No clutter \u2014 a lone block shows nothing.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 pull cells into formulas, and pick scattered cells: while you are typing a formula (start with =), click a cell to drop in its reference (=A1), drag to grab a range (=A1:B3), or hold Cmd/Ctrl and click to add more (=A1,B2). Separately, hold Cmd/Ctrl while clicking to select several cells that are NOT next to each other, then Delete or format them together.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 adjustment layers: add a colour-grade layer (brightness, contrast, warmth, saturation or grayscale) that adjusts everything beneath it, with a slider for how strong it is. Stack a few to combine looks, tap the eye to toggle one off and compare, and reorder it to change what it affects \u2014 your drawing layers are never altered, the grade just sits on top. Add one from the Layers panel with the new \u201cAdjust\u201d button (the panel now scrolls when you have lots of layers).\n\u2022 Edit mode \u2014 friendlier setting names: when you edit an applet, a block\'s settings now read in plain words (e.g. \u201cTwo-finger hold (ms)\u201d, \u201cOpen on a blank canvas\u201d) instead of code-style keys.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 a whole Screen now snaps to the grid WHILE you drag it, not just when you let go: drag a frame and everything inside it glides to the grid together, keeping its spacing, so screens line up cleanly as you move them.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 resizing is a real selection box now: select a block and you get eight handles (four corners + four sides) like a design tool. Drag any handle to resize from that edge, and sizes snap to the grid too (the grid button turns snapping off for free resizing). The old single corner dot is gone.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 connector arrows that stick: drag from a block\'s connect dot onto another block and the arrow latches onto the nearest corner or side and STAYS there when you move or resize either block (like draw.io), instead of floating to the centre.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 reorder the widget drawer: press and HOLD a chip in the drawer, then drag it left/right to put the widgets in the order you like \u2014 and the new order sticks across reloads.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 type to edit, and Delete clears your selection: with a cell highlighted just start typing to overwrite it (no double-click needed), and press Delete or Backspace to wipe every selected cell at once. Shift-click still extends the selection from the active cell.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 the widget drawer moved off the bottom into a side panel: on a wide screen it is a left rail you can collapse; on a phone it stays out of the way and pulls in from a tab on the left edge, so the canvas gets the whole screen. The tools (Select/Draw/Text) stay on the canvas itself.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 a real toolset on the canvas: a tool palette (top-left) to pick Select, Draw or Text, with pen colour + thickness for drawing. A live preview now shows exactly where a dragged widget will land before you let go, and a trash bin appears while you drag a block \u2014 drop it there to delete. (Draw/text marks pan and zoom with the diagram.)\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 drag widgets onto the canvas, and it fills the screen now: the drawer along the bottom is draggable \u2014 grab a widget and drop it exactly where you want on the canvas (it lands there, snapped to the grid), or just tap it to drop one in at a default spot like before. The canvas now fills the whole area between the toolbar and the drawer, so there is room to work and page-scrolling no longer fights your dragging; zoom and pan with the controls on the right or two-finger / trackpad. The same drawer works in the Maker and standalone Wireframe apps and in Design.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 blocks snap to line up with each other, in every direction: while you drag a block it gently locks on when its edge or centre lines up with a nearby block \u2014 left/right and top/bottom both \u2014 and a coloured guide line shows the match so you can see what it caught. A faint grid now shows behind the canvas while snapping is on, so things land tidy. Tap the grid button to turn snapping (and the grid) off \u2014 then blocks stay exactly where you drop them, no nudging, for fine placement.\n\u2022 Wireframes look like real components now: a button looks like a button, an input like a field, a list like rows, and so on. Tap the view button in the canvas controls to switch the whole diagram between three looks \u2014 Themed (real components), Wireframe (plain black-outline boxes), and Colored (the simple tinted blocks). Works on any diagram, wireframe or flowchart.\n\u2022 Sheets \u2014 fills the screen and scrolls properly: the grid now uses the full page height instead of stopping halfway, with a scrollbar pinned to the right edge and (new) one along the bottom, each in its own reserved track so they no longer float over your cells. Drag either track to scroll, or keep using the mouse wheel / two-finger pan.\n\u2022 Web \u2014 no more accidental browser back/forward: a two-finger sideways swipe (and pinch-zoom of the whole page) now stays inside the app instead of navigating away or zooming the page, and an over-scroll can\'t bump the app off-screen.\n\u2022 A preset project to review: open the Design app and you\'ll find a worked example \u2014 a Habit Tracker designed across the tabs (Wireframe = the home screen, Flowchart = the daily loop, Brief = what it is + the features, and a data model). Edit it freely, or tap Prepare for Claude to send your own.\n\u2022 Sketch on top of a diagram: on a wireframe, flowchart or relationships diagram, tap the pencil ("Sketch on top") in the canvas controls, then draw freehand or switch to the text tool to tap-and-type a label right over it. Undo and clear are there too, and your marks pan/zoom with the diagram.\n\u2022 Diagrams \u2014 two-finger pan: drag with two fingers to move around a diagram or wireframe (pinch still zooms, one finger still selects and drags). On the web, a two-finger swipe no longer flips the page back or forward by mistake.\n\u2022 Design is now your one diagram hub: the Flowchart and Relationships tabs are the Diagram and Schema Map apps embedded right inside (each with its own copy). Those apps \u2014 plus Architecture \u2014 are tidied away as reusable components: off the home shelf, but search still finds and opens them.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 tune the pinch-vs-draw timing: the brief window that decides whether a second finger keeps your stroke or starts a zoom is now a setting (default a quarter-second), adjustable in the app builder.\n\u2022 Wireframe \u2014 drag like a design tool: drag a screen frame and the blocks on it move with it (anything sitting inside a bigger box is carried along). Bigger boxes sit behind, so you can grab a block on top or the frame\'s edge. Positions snap to a grid for tidiness \u2014 tap the grid button to turn snapping off and place freely.\n\u2022 Reusable Wireframe: there\'s now a standalone Wireframe app that other apps can inherit \u2014 each app that uses it gets its OWN wireframe (its own copy of the data), just like each app has its own sketches.\n\u2022 Hidden apps: an app can be marked hidden \u2014 it stays off your home shelf so it doesn\'t clutter it, but still turns up (greyed, and openable) when you search. Good for reusable building blocks like the Wireframe.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 pinch-to-zoom feels right now: once two fingers start a zoom, the canvas won\'t draw until you lift both \u2014 no stray marks on release, and lifting to one finger won\'t suddenly draw. A finger that lands after you\'ve been drawing keeps your stroke instead of erasing it. Trackpad pinch-zoom works in the browser too.\n\u2022 Design \u2014 Brief for Claude: a new Brief tab pulls your whole request into one place. Say whether it\'s a new app or a change, list the features / changes / questions (with a priority), and sketch the data model \u2014 then tap "Prepare for Claude" to bundle it all (this brief + your wireframe + flow + data model + notes) into one package on your clipboard. Paste it to Claude and it knows exactly what to build.\n\u2022 Taskly fixed \u2014 its localized stat cards now render (engine i18n fix).\n\u2022 Workboard + Gather added to the launcher.\n\u2022 Map choropleth + misc engine improvements.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 the toolbar now shows which layer you\'re drawing on (e.g. "Layer 2 \xb7 2/3"), so new marks always land where you expect and you can tell which layer the show/hide eye affects.\n\u2022 Design \u2014 turn a wireframe into an app: sketch a screen on the Wireframe tab (a frame with buttons, inputs, text, a list), then tap "Build app from wireframe". Each frame becomes a screen and each block becomes a widget, and it opens in the builder so you can finish it \u2014 your sketch becomes a real, editable app.\n\u2022 Applet Studio \u2014 make real apps: open Studio, start a project (give it a name + icon), then tap it and choose "Open builder". That mints a real app, drops it on your home shelf, and opens the visual builder so you can add screens, widgets and button logic \u2014 no code. Your edits save to that app and it relaunches like any other.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 layers: build a drawing up in layers. Add a layer for lines, squiggles or notes, show/hide or reorder each one, and rename or delete them; new drawings start with one simple layer.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 pinch to zoom: two fingers zoom and pan the canvas (mouse wheel on desktop) while one finger still draws \u2014 no more mode switch.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 copy a region: pick the Select tool, drag a box, and "Copy region" puts just that part on the clipboard.\n\u2022 Shotpad is retired \u2014 Sketchpad now does everything it did (paste a picture, mark it up, copy a region), so there is one drawing app, not two.\n\u2022 New app \u2014 Sheets: a real spreadsheet. Type =formulas (=B1*C1, =SUM(D1:D3), =AVERAGE(B1:C1)); cells show the computed value, editing shows the formula. Drag across cells to select a block, \u2318C/\u2318V to copy-paste it, and \u2318D/\u2318R to fill a formula down or right with references that shift per cell ($A$1 stays pinned). Right-click (or long-press) a selection for copy, cut, paste, fill, move/insert/delete rows and columns, and clear. Format selected cells from the bar above the grid: bold, italic, text color, highlight, and font size. Sort by a column header; shift-click more headers to sort by several at once (sort by A, then B within A). Tapping anywhere in a cell now selects it (not just on the text), and selecting a cell highlights its row number rather than shading the whole row. The grid fills the whole screen, and pasting a block wider than the sheet adds the extra columns for you. On a touchscreen, drag one finger to select a range of cells and use two fingers to scroll the sheet. Zoom the grid 50\u2013250% with the zoom control in the toolbar, and the sheet now uses the full screen height. Sheets is also available in 9 languages (Settings \u2192 Language).\n\u2022 Share an app by code: long-press any app \u2192 "Copy share code" puts the whole app on your clipboard. On another device, long-press \u2192 "Paste applet" to drop it in \u2014 no file, and it works on the web app too.\n\u2022 Builder \u2014 pick from dropdowns: when you set a button\'s action or an "only if" condition, the table, column, and field boxes are now menus of your real tables and columns \u2014 no more typing names exactly right.\n\u2022 Nested apps: an app can now show another whole app live inside one of its tabs. The first example: open Design \u2192 Relationships and you\'re looking at the schema_map app embedded right there \u2014 same data, edits sync back.\n\u2022 New app \u2014 Design: a place to plan a UI. Block out a screen on the Wireframe tab (frame, nav, buttons, inputs, text, image), map a process or data flow on the Flowchart tab, sketch freehand on the Sketch tab, and keep notes \u2014 plus the live Relationships tab above.\n\u2022 Taskly \u2014 recurring tasks: give a task a Repeat (open it \u2192 Advanced \u2192 "Repeat", e.g. d:1 daily, w:1 weekly, m:1 monthly). When you complete it, the next one appears automatically with the date moved forward. There\'s a sample "Weekly review" task to try it on.\n\u2022 Vertier \u2014 Discover is now SHARED online: publish a tier list and it shows up for everyone; the feed and vote counts come from the server. Sign in to publish or vote (one vote per person per creation, with a daily budget); browsing is open to all.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 you\'re in control of saving: drawings are no longer saved automatically. An "Unsaved"/"Saved" badge shows where you stand, a Save button writes your work, and switching away from an unsaved drawing asks first (Save / Discard / Cancel).\n\u2022 Builder \u2014 automated rules without code: a button\'s action editor now has an "only if\u2026" step. Build a condition (field / is / value, with and/or/not) and choose what to do when it matches (and optionally otherwise) \u2014 all from menus, no JSON.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 paste a picture and draw on it: press \u2318/Ctrl+V (or the paste button) to drop a clipboard image onto the canvas. Nudge it into place, then start drawing \u2014 it locks in as pixels and your ink goes on top.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 copy with the keyboard: \u2318/Ctrl+C copies the whole drawing (picture + ink) to the clipboard, ready to paste anywhere.\n\u2022 Sketchpad \u2014 tidier screen: the folder list is tucked behind the menu (\u2630) button, and the app opens straight onto a fresh blank canvas.\n\u2022 Taskly \u2014 process templates: a new Templates tab. Tap a template (e.g. "Onboard a new client") to copy it and all its steps into your Tasks; build your own under "Build templates". Two starters included.\n\u2022 Taskly \u2014 quick stage: on the Board, tap a task to advance it (To do \u2192 Doing \u2192 Done). Reaching Done now stamps the date automatically \u2014 the first of the new automated rules.\n\u2022 Builder \u2014 custom button logic: editing a button\'s action now shows a guided picker ("when tapped \u2192 do X", chain "+ then" steps) instead of raw JSON, with a "uses the network" badge on actions that go online.\n\u2022 New app \u2014 Puzzles: slide a 15-puzzle or solve a co-op pattern board. Every move is shared, so open it in a room (\u22ee \u2192 Share) and solve together live on different phones.\n\u2022 Vertier \u2014 Discover + voting: publish your tier list for the community, and a Discover feed ranks everyone\'s creations by votes. Upvote/downvote with a daily vote budget. (Shared online voting is coming next.)\n\u2022 Games: much faster now \u2014 moves and deals no longer stutter as you play.\n\u2022 Games: the Runner mini-game loads again (was showing an error).\n\u2022 New app \u2014 Vertier: compare and rank anything. Sort footballers by any stat, see two players head-to-head, or drag them into S/A/B/C/D tiers. Built to work for any list (players, phones, films); your own collections and community voting are coming next.\n\u2022 Sketchpad: a folder sidebar \u2014 browse your drawings in a tree, make folders, and rename or delete from the rail; deleted items go to a recoverable Trash. (The file browser is a new building block apps can reuse.)\n\u2022 Sketchpad: draw shapes \u2014 new line, arrow, rectangle and ellipse tools alongside the freehand pen, with a Fill toggle for boxes/ovals.\n\u2022 Sketchpad: add text \u2014 pick the new text tool, tap anywhere, and type straight onto your drawing; the words save and export with the image, and the brush slider sets their size.\n\u2022 Sketchpad: hold Shift for straight lines \u2014 lines and arrows snap to perfectly horizontal, vertical or 45\xb0, and rectangles/ellipses snap to a true square or circle.\n\u2022 Sketchpad: export a drawing \u2014 "Copy" puts it on the clipboard and "Save PNG" saves it to a folder (auto-cropped to your sketch).\n\u2022 Your existing drawings still open and edit unchanged.\n\u2022 Games: real playing cards \u2014 proper suits, pip layouts and card backs.\n\u2022 Card games decluttered \u2014 the felt table, not number tiles.\n\u2022 Faster card/dice rendering.',k,k,k,k,k,g.z,k,k,k),B.aDn,r],f),B.O,k,B.h,B.p,0,B.m),k,k,k,k,k,!1,B.Y)
